  "body": "**Link to problem area**:\r\nhttps://matrix.org/docs/spec/client_server/r0.6.1#get-matrix-media-r0-download-servername-mediaid\r\nand\r\nhttps://matrix.org/docs/spec/client_server/r0.6.1#get-matrix-media-r0-download-servername-mediaid-filename\r\n\r\n> **Response headers:**\r\n> \r\n> Parameter | Type | Description\r\n> -- | -- | --\r\n> Content-Disposition | `string` | The `fileName` requested or the name of the file that was previously uploaded, if set.\r\n\r\n**Issue**\r\nWhat is wrong?\r\nIt suggests to write:\r\n```\r\ncontent-disposition: Screenshot_2021-05-29_12.24.32.png\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe result is that people with clients that show these not inline but as links that you are expected to open in a browser. This is probably most common when bridging to IRC.\r\n\r\n**Expected behaviour**\r\n```\r\ncontent-disposition: inline; filename=Screenshot_2021-05-29_12.24.32.png\r\n```\r\n\r\nSynapse already does the right thing. Conduit does not, and that's how @timokoesters and I found the issue.\r\n\r\nLink to spec: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6266#section-4 \r\n\r\nYes, it's a seperate document, so it's technically not even the HTTP spec we're violating but the Internet is weird like that.",
